-Conversely a state $s$ yields an acyclic set whenever the sides opposite the chosen corners form a forest with one corner of $t_0$ in each tree (Section~\ref{sec:corners}, Figure~\ref{fig:cube}(d)--(f)).
-A second state $r$ that differs from $s$ in every triangle orients these opposite sides and reduces the condition to ``no cycle''.
