 \subsection*{History and sources}
-Tait conjectured in 1884 that cubic $3$-connected planar graphs are Hamiltonian \cite{tait1884}, and Tutte disproved this in 1946 with a non-bipartite example \cite{tutte1946}.
-Barnette asked whether bipartiteness rescues the statement \cite{barnette1969}.
-Before 2026 the conjecture was known when all faces have four or six sides \cite{goodey1975}, under conditions on two of the three face colour classes \cite{feder-subi2006}, when all faces have at most eight sides \cite{schnieders2025}, and by computer up to 90 vertices \cite{hmm1985,bgm2021}.
-In a preprint dated 24 September 2026, OpenAI proved Theorem~\ref{thm:main} \cite{openai2026}.
-The objects of the proof are theirs: pairs of states, the opposite sides, the cycles along which two states differ, the disk identity, the cancellation for a fixed second state, the regrouping by cycles, the flow argument and the gluing.
-States are the corner choices of Tutte's theory of trinity \cite{tutte1975}, see also \cite{hine-kalman2018}.
-The target, two induced trees in $\T$, is Stein's reformulation \cite{stein1971} in the form of Alt, Payne, Schmidt and Wood \cite[Lemma~5]{apsw2016}.
-Two expositions followed.
-Li \cite{li2026} works in $G$ and replaces OpenAI's exponential sum by two sign-reversing involutions; his counts of crossings between half-rays from white vertices and matching edges are the same objects as the heights of Section~\ref{sec:pairs}.
-An unsigned manuscript \cite{constructive2026} uses a real sign and an integer polynomial, describes partners by outdegrees, and gives an exact criterion for the existence of a pair and an enumeration algorithm.
-
-Our account differs from \cite{openai2026} in four places, each proved in full below.
-The disk identity is stated as ``one more white face than black'', with a short lemma that makes Euler's formula applicable to the inside of a cycle, and acyclicity of the final set is read off from it.
-The sign of a pair is a product of local signs.
-The weights are heights read along paths of faces, which are OpenAI's weights for one choice of their circulations; this removes the spokes, the sliding argument of \cite[Section~5.3]{openai2026} and the exponential sum.
-The tree on the faces that selects the final set is obtained from the opposite sides by planar duality in $\T$; in $G$ it is Li's black dual trace \cite[Section~6]{li2026} with one extra leaf.
